{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-46aae89f-b116-472b-9f3a-219f0cc0a3d1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a26267e7ba3bb200","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8008-n6x5i9hx/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8008-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8008\n\nInference-Aware Autoscaling for llm-d — standalone KEDA primitives for LLMInferenceService.\n\nStaff Engineer / SME Input section is empty (template placeholder only) — no overrides to apply.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8008-E001 | Standalone KEDA autoscaling path for LLMInferenceService in kserve llmisvc-controller | Implementation | Serving team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8008-E002 | KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService in odh-model-controller | Implementation | Serving team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8008-E003 | Autoscaling decision guide and configuration documentation for LLMInferenceService | Implementation (docs-authoring) | Documentation team | P0 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[RHAISTRAT-8008-E001 Standalone KEDA path in llmisvc-controller]\n    E002[RHAISTRAT-8008-E002 TriggerAuthentication reconciler in odh-model-controller]\n    E003[RHAISTRAT-8008-E003 Autoscaling documentation]\n    E001 --> E002\n    E001 --> E003\n    E002 --> E003\n```\n\nCritical path: E001 → E002 → E003 (length 3).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | E001 lands the standalone KEDA spec in the LLMInferenceService v1alpha2 API (ScalingSpec `keda` field, CEL validation). The odh-model-controller reconciler's code builds against those API types and reconciles CRs carrying the new fields — a build-against API/CRD artifact, not a configuration reference. |\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes the implemented API and behavior; blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must cover the OpenShift TriggerAuthentication flow delivered by the odh-model-controller work. |\n\nNotes on edges deliberately NOT drawn:\n\n- No Investigation epics were created (see Unknowns Analysis below). All three strategy open questions fail the \"does the answer change which downstream epics exist or what they do\" test — they change content or in-epic scope, not epic structure.\n- Upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 is modeled as a precondition with a defined fallback (carry the patch downstream), per Rule 21 third-party-resolves case — an acceptance criterion on E001, not a separate upstream epic.\n- No konflux-onboarding / repo-onboarding / license-validation epics: kserve and odh-model-controller are existing platform components with architecture context files; the change extends existing code in already-onboarded repos.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------|\n| HLR-1: Standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers without WVA | P0 | E001, E002 |\n| HLR-2: Mutual exclusivity of WVA and standalone KEDA paths (CEL validation) | P0 | E001 |\n| HLR-3: Scale-to-zero via KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0` (both paths) | P0 | E001 |\n| HLR-4: Scale-from-zero (standalone KEDA via user-configured triggers) | P0 | E001 |\n| HLR-5: Independent prefill/decode scaling in disaggregated deployments | P0 | E001 |\n| HLR-6: Scaling events with signal attribution | P1 | E001 |\n| HLR-7: Decision-guide documentation covering both paths | P1 | E003 |\n\nFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n\nHLR-6 note: the WVA-path half of this HLR (WVA events) belongs to RHAISTRAT-7670, where WVA-mediated autoscaling remains the technical path; this decomposition covers the standalone-KEDA half (ScaledObject events / scaling status conditions) in E001. The P1 scaling-observability work is bundled in E001 rather than split: it shares the same controller status-condition surface as the P0 path implementation, so it is incidental to the P0 work, not a deferrable feature on a separate surface.\n\n## Component Mappings\n\nCanonical component names are taken verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`. Non-obvious mappings:\n\n| Strategy component | Canonical component | Note |\n|--------------------|---------------------|------|\n| KServe (llmisvc-controller) | Serving Orchestration | No literal \"KServe\" entry in the canonical list; the llmisvc-controller is the serving orchestration layer for LLMInferenceService. |\n| odh-model-controller | Model Runtimes | No literal \"odh-model-controller\" entry; the controller manages model-serving runtime deployment concerns (including KEDA auth for serving workloads). |\n| Documentation | Documentation | Verbatim match. |\n\n## Unknowns Analysis (Step 3)\n\nAll unknowns were collected from the Open Questions table, Risks, and Assumptions before evaluation:\n\n1. **Does upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 require downstream adaptation beyond the authentication context?** (Owner: KServe team) — The answer changes in-epic scope (possible OpenShift RBAC/TLS adjustments) but not which epics exist: the adaptation work naturally falls inside E001's integration scope (rebase or carry-patch), and E002 already owns the authentication context. Resolved by doing the rebase — captured as an acceptance criterion on E001, not an Investigation epic.\n2. **Target p50/p95 for scale-from-zero time** (Owner: llm-d team) — A product SLO that determines documentation content (headroom pre-scaling recommendations), not epic structure. Captured as an input/AC on E003; flagged as a health warning (external input with a 60-second p50 reference from the RFE).\n3. **Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet-scale deployments** (Owner: Performance/PSAP team) — Documentation content, not structure. Captured as an AC on E003; flagged as a health warning (external PSAP input).\n\nAssumptions marked \"needs validation\" (KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0` pause/resume behavior; EPP flow control metrics availability at zero replicas) are validations of behavior during implementation — failure means fix-and-retry, not different epics. Captured as acceptance criteria on E001 (scale-to-zero and metric-outage ACs from the strategy).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Pending process gates**: Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and other Prerequisites are all \"Pending human review\" — the strategy is in Refinement. Decomposition assumes sign-off; a materially different review outcome would require re-decomposition.\n- **Upstream timing risk**: E001 depends on kserve/kserve#5839 (open, assigned). Fallback (carry the patch downstream with backport) is an explicit AC on E001; if the PR is rejected rather than delayed, E001's approach needs rework — monitor upstream.\n- **External inputs to documentation**: E003 content depends on inputs the Documentation team does not control (scale-from-zero SLO from the llm-d team; Prometheus capacity guidance from Performance/PSAP). If unavailable, E003 ships with the RFE-referenced 60-second p50 and a capacity-tuning section marked as guidance pending PSAP validation.\n- **Docs priority**: E003 is P0 solely via the docs-authoring priority rule (strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0); its underlying HLR is P1. Not a priority inversion — rule-driven — but planners should treat it as P1-value work sequenced last.\n- **HLR-6 split ownership**: the WVA-events half of the observability HLR is owned by RHAISTRAT-7670; only the standalone-KEDA half is covered here.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nEvaluated per Step 5.5 for the uncertain-timing external dependency (kserve/kserve#5839):\n\n- Partial value without upstream landing is limited to carrying the patch downstream — which delivers the full capability, not a tier. The odh-model-controller work (E002) builds against the API delivered by E001 and cannot ship user value ahead of it; documentation (E003) describes the built surface.\n- **Conclusion: no Tier 1 / Tier 2 split.** The fallback (carry patch downstream) is recorded as an acceptance criterion on E001, matching the strategy's stated mitigation. If upstream lands on time, E001 simply consumes the rebase instead.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":36265,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9cbc04831ea090fb","agent_id":"a7506effac068fb42","session_id":"975609a4-9996-4dcf-a42c-1d087396c61b","uuid":"5b236316-4e6c-419d-b1b5-b93b984c294f","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:30:07.406Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8008"}
