{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-f2f3b987-a701-47c0-a75f-e45013a8c85a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-bd7ba384a0f70f59","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8008-n6x5i9hx/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8008-E001.md","content":"# Standalone KEDA autoscaling path for LLMInferenceService in kserve llmisvc-controller\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers standalone KEDA autoscaling as a first-class path for LLMInferenceService in the kserve llmisvc-controller, so users can configure KEDA triggers directly in the LLMInferenceService spec without deploying WVA. It follows the upstream design in kserve/kserve#5646 and its implementing PR kserve/kserve#5839, bringing LLMInferenceService to parity with v1beta1 InferenceService standalone KEDA support.\n\n## Scope\n\n- API type changes in `pkg/apis/serving/v1alpha2/llm_inference_service_types.go`: a standalone KEDA spec at the ScalingSpec level containing `pollingInterval`, `cooldownPeriod`, and a `triggers` field for user-defined KEDA trigger definitions (type, metadata map).\n- CEL validation update: relax the rule that currently requires `has(self.wva)` on the ScalingSpec to require at least one of `wva` or `keda`, with at-most-one (mutual exclusivity) enforced and clear error messages.\n- Validation changes in `pkg/apis/serving/v1alpha2/llm_inference_service_validation.go`: mutual exclusivity between the two paths, trigger completeness, and actuator consistency for disaggregated deployments (both prefill and decode phases must use the same scaling path, per architecture overlay 0011). Mutual exclusivity with static `spec.replicas` is preserved.\n- Controller changes in `pkg/controller/v1alpha2/llmisvc/scaling.go`: branch on which path is configured — the standalone KEDA path creates a ScaledObject using user-provided triggers and skips VariantAutoscaling CR creation entirely. The v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA reconciler at `pkg/controller/v1beta1/inferenceservice/reconcilers/keda/keda_reconciler.go` is the reference implementation for trigger construction (Resource, External, and PodMetric trigger types).\n- Scale-to-zero in the standalone path via KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0`, releasing GPU capacity when deployments are idle; scale-from-zero via user-configured triggers (e.g., Prometheus query on EPP queue metrics).\n- Independent prefill/decode scaling for disaggregated deployments via phase-specific ScaledObjects.\n- Scaling status conditions and KEDA ScaledObject events for the standalone path so scaling decisions are visible to operators with signal attribution.\n- Integration with the upstream kserve code base: rebase on kserve/kserve#5839 once merged; if the PR is delayed, carry the patch downstream in the midstream/downstream kserve fork with a backport path (the stated strategy mitigation).\n- Integration testing across the standalone KEDA path for both monolithic and disaggregated topologies, including scale-to-zero, scale-from-zero, and metric-outage behavior.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given an LLMInferenceService with the standalone KEDA spec configured with user-defined triggers and no WVA deployed, when a trigger condition is met (e.g., Prometheus query threshold exceeded), then the deployment scales up, measured by KEDA ScaledObject status showing active and Deployment replica count increasing.\n- Given an LLMInferenceService with both the WVA and standalone KEDA specs set, when the CR is submitted, then the API server rejects it with a CEL validation error indicating mutual exclusivity.\n- Given an LLMInferenceService with the standalone KEDA spec and no WVA spec, when the llmisvc-controller reconciles, then no VariantAutoscaling CR is created and a ScaledObject with the user-provided triggers is created, measured by `kubectl get variantautoscalings` returning empty and `kubectl get scaledobjects` returning a ScaledObject with the user's triggers.\n- Given an LLMInferenceService with KEDA and `idleReplicaCount: 0`, when no requests are received for the cooldown period, then the deployment scales to zero, measured by Deployment replica count reaching 0 and KEDA ScaledObject status showing idle. (Validates the strategy assumption that KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0` correctly pauses and resumes scaling for LLM inference workloads — failure means fix-and-retry within this epic.)\n- Given a disaggregated LLMInferenceService with separate scaling configs, when prefill pods reach saturation while decode pods remain below threshold, then only the prefill pool scales up, measured by independent scaling resource status for prefill and decode roles.\n- Given a deployment with autoscaling active, when Prometheus metrics become unavailable, then the deployment holds its current replica count, measured by replica count remaining stable during the metric outage. (Also validates availability of EPP flow control queue metrics when pods are at zero replicas — the recommended scale-from-zero trigger source.)\n- Static deployments with no autoscaling configured continue to serve identically, measured by no VariantAutoscaling, HPA, or ScaledObject resources created for deployments without `spec.scaling`.\n- Backwards compatibility: existing LLMInferenceService CRs with `spec.scaling.wva` continue to reconcile unchanged, measured by an unchanged WVA-path integration test suite passing.\n- The upstream integration path is decided and executed: either kserve/kserve#5839 is merged and rebased into the midstream/downstream kserve fork, or the patch is carried downstream with a documented backport plan; any OpenShift-specific adaptation required beyond the authentication context (e.g., RBAC or TLS adjustments) is identified and implemented.\n- Scaling decision events for the standalone path are emitted via KEDA ScaledObject events and reflected in scaling status conditions.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): LLMInferenceService supports standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers without requiring WVA deployment — API, validation, and controller work in this epic.\n- HLR-2 (P0): Mutual exclusivity of the two autoscaling paths with CEL validation enforcing at-least-one and at-most-one.\n- HLR-3 (P0): Scale-to-zero via KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0` (standalone path; the KEDA actuator is shared by both paths).\n- HLR-4 (P0): Scale-from-zero via user-configured triggers in the standalone path.\n- HLR-5 (P0): Disaggregated prefill and decode pools scale independently.\n- HLR-6 (P1): Scaling events visible to operators with signal attribution for the standalone path (KEDA ScaledObject events). The WVA-path half of this HLR is owned by RHAISTRAT-7670 (WVA-mediated autoscaling remains the technical path there).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":38829,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9cbc04831ea090fb","agent_id":"a7506effac068fb42","session_id":"975609a4-9996-4dcf-a42c-1d087396c61b","uuid":"91256dba-fdd6-4328-afb6-d81dc9777127","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:30:29.019Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8008"}
