{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-4eb2e9be-ad10-445b-ba3b-b4474e568ff9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-855bae8618925708","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8008-n6x5i9hx/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8008-E003.md","content":"# Autoscaling decision guide and configuration documentation for LLMInferenceService\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the Red Hat documentation for LLMInferenceService autoscaling: a decision guide covering WVA-mediated autoscaling, standalone KEDA, and static allocation, plus configuration recipes, troubleshooting, and a supported configuration matrix, so operators can choose and configure the right autoscaling path for their workload shape.\n\n## Scope\n\nContent outline (topics to be authored in the RHOAI documentation set):\n\n- **Decision guide**: WVA-mediated autoscaling vs. standalone KEDA vs. static allocation, with use-case-based recommendations (e.g., inference-aware WVA signals for operators who want computed optimal replica counts; trigger-based standalone KEDA for operators with existing KEDA infrastructure and custom scaling triggers such as CPU utilization, custom Prometheus queries, or external queue depth). Addresses the risk that two scaling paths in a single CRD confuse operators.\n- **Configuration recipes per workload shape**: standalone KEDA trigger configuration (polling interval, cooldown period, triggers), mutual exclusivity rules and their CEL error messages, and the example scenario of a coding assistant deployment scaling prefill and decode pools during business hours and releasing GPUs overnight.\n- **Scale-to-zero and scale-from-zero guidance**: KEDA as the required actuator for scale-to-zero (`idleReplicaCount: 0`), recommended scale-from-zero trigger configurations using EPP flow control queue metrics, and example ScaledObject templates (mitigating the risk that misconfigured triggers fail the zero-to-one transition and leave requests waiting indefinitely).\n- **Authenticated Prometheus/Thanos triggers on OpenShift**: instructions to reference the TriggerAuthentication resource in KEDA trigger definitions for authenticated queries, per the odh-model-controller authentication flow.\n- **Troubleshooting**: diagnosis paths for failed scale-up, failed zero-to-one transitions, and metric outages (deployment holds replica count).\n- **Supported configuration matrix**: which combinations of scaling path, actuator, and topology (monolithic vs. disaggregated) are supported, including that both phases in a disaggregated deployment must use the same scaling path and that HPA is supported only as an actuator within the WVA path.\n- **Prerequisites**: KEDA (Custom Metrics Autoscaler) as a documented prerequisite with installation guidance referencing the GitOps repository (ODH-ADR-0007) — RHOAI does not auto-install KEDA (overlay 0008) — and User Workload Monitoring enablement for the metrics pipeline.\n- **Cold start expectations**: realistic scale-from-zero (trigger to serving) time expectations referencing the model size, storage backend, and GPU initialization factors, with headroom configuration recommendations for latency-sensitive workloads; reference the fast model startup RFEs (RHAIRFE-7753, RHAIRFE-7599, RHAIRFE-7139) for related work.\n- **Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet-scale deployments**: KEDA polling intervals and their Prometheus load implications at scale, based on Performance/PSAP team input (see notes).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The decision guide covers all three autoscaling paths (WVA-mediated, standalone KEDA, static allocation) with use-case-based recommendations, measured by technical review against the implemented behavior confirming each recommendation matches the shipped API and controller semantics.\n- Configuration recipes exist for standalone KEDA triggers, scale-to-zero, scale-from-zero with EPP queue metrics (including example ScaledObject templates), and disaggregated prefill/decode scaling, measured by a reviewer successfully applying a recipe against a test cluster.\n- Documentation instructs operators to reference the TriggerAuthentication resource for authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift, measured by technical review confirming consistency with the implemented odh-model-controller authentication flow.\n- Troubleshooting topics cover failed scale-up, failed zero-to-one transitions, and metric-outage behavior, measured by technical review against implemented behavior.\n- The supported configuration matrix is complete and accurate, measured by technical review against the shipped CEL validation rules (at-least-one, at-most-one, phase consistency, mutual exclusivity with static replicas).\n- KEDA installation is documented as a prerequisite with GitOps repository guidance, measured by technical review against ODH-ADR-0007 and overlay 0008 policy.\n- Technically reviewed against implementation: all autoscaling documentation topics are reviewed by engineering against the implemented controller and authentication behavior before publication.\n- Scale-from-zero time expectations and headroom recommendations reflect the agreed p50/p95 targets (RFE references a 60-second p50; final targets owned by the llm-d team) and Prometheus capacity guidance reflects Performance/PSAP input; where an input is not yet available, the topic ships with the referenced threshold and is flagged for update.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-7 (P1): Well-lit path documentation covers both autoscaling paths with decision guide, configuration recipes per workload shape, and troubleshooting.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Inputs outside the Documentation team's control: the scale-from-zero p50/p95 target (llm-d team) and fleet-scale Prometheus capacity guidance (Performance/PSAP team). If unresolved when documentation is authored, publish with the RFE-referenced 60-second p50 and mark cap