{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4274e94b03d2391","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8008-E002\n3\ttitle: KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService in odh-model-controller\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8008\n5\tcomponent: Model Runtimes\n6\tteam: Serving team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8008-E001\n11\tai_signals:\n12\t  change_specificity: 1\n13\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n14\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n15\t  existing_foundation: 1\n16\t  open_questions: 0\n17\t  external_dependency: 0\n18\t  human_process_gates: 0\n19\t  repo_access: 1\n20\t  architecture_claims: 1\n21\timplementation_type: null\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 6\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService in odh-model-controller\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tThis epic delivers the downstream KEDA TriggerAuthentication flow in odh-model-controller so that LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA Prometheus-type triggers can query Thanos/Prometheus with an authenticated context on OpenShift. On OpenShift, KEDA ScaledObjects cannot query Thanos/Prometheus without authentication; this epic creates the per-namespace authentication resources for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA, mirroring the flow that already exists for v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA autoscaling.\n35\t\n36\t## Scope\n37\t\n38\t- A parallel reconciler flow in odh-model-controller for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA with Prometheus-type triggers, following the existing `kserve_keda_reconciler` pattern at `internal/controller/serving/reconcilers/kserve_keda_reconciler.go`.\n39\t- Per-namespace authentication resources, matching the existing reference implementation's resource set: a dedicated ServiceAccount (`inference-prometheus-auth`), a ServiceAccountToken Secret (`kubernetes.io/service-account-token` type), a Role/RoleBinding for metrics access, and a KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR supplying bearer token and CA cert to KEDA's Prometheus triggers.\n40\t- Owner reference management and lifecycle cleanup consistent with the existing reconciler (resources garbage-collected with the owning LLMInferenceService).\n41\t- Reuse of the existing reconciler's conventions for resource naming so operators see a consistent authentication surface across v1beta1 InferenceService and LLMInferenceService.\n42\t- Identification and implementation of any OpenShift-specific RBAC or TLS adjustments this flow requires beyond the existing v1beta1 pattern (the strategy's open question on downstream adaptation is resolved here for the authentication context; TLS 1.2+ via the TriggerAuthentication CR per the security NFR).\n43\t\n44\t## Acceptance Criteria\n45\t\n46\t- Given an LLMInferenceService with the standalone KEDA spec configured with Prometheus-type triggers, when the odh-model-controller reconciles, then the per-namespace authentication resources are created: ServiceAccount, ServiceAccountToken Secret, Role/RoleBinding, and KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR, measured by `kubectl get` returning each resource in the target namespace.\n47\t- Given an LLMInferenceService using the KEDA TriggerAuthentication flow, when KEDA evaluates a Prometheus trigger, then the query succeeds with ServiceAccount token authentication over TLS 1.2+, measured by the ScaledObject reporting the trigger as active and no authentication errors in KEDA operator logs.\n48\t- Given an LLMInferenceService is deleted, when the reconciler performs lifecycle cleanup, then the owned authentication resources are removed, measured by owner-reference garbage collection leaving no orphans.\n49\t- LLMInferenceService CRs without standalone KEDA Prometheus triggers produce no authentication resources, measured by no TriggerAuthentication CRs in namespaces without qualifying CRs.\n50\t- Existing v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA authentication behavior is unchanged, measured by the existing `kserve_keda_reconciler` integration tests passing unmodified.\n51\t\n52\t## HLR Traceability\n53\t\n54\t- HLR-1 (P0): LLMInferenceService supports standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers without requiring WVA deployment — this epic supplies the OpenShift authentication context that makes the primary supported trigger source (Prometheus/Thanos queries) functional.\n55\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad0da4a90b4435dd","agent_id":"af4153aabb1820807","session_id":"975609a4-9996-4dcf-a42c-1d087396c61b","uuid":"7eb282e8-ace3-4555-9032-6e96bdd87079","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:32:28.609Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4274e94b03d2391","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8008 decomposition"}
