ne KEDA primitives) into 3 epics: E001 (kserve llmisvc-controller standalone KEDA path), E002 (odh-model-controller TriggerAuthentication reconciler), E003 (docs-authoring). Triage: null (full decomposition) — multi-epic output is expected and correct. Contract validator: OK (all mechanically decidable rules pass: body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance). No critical or major issues found; four minor observations recorded.\n40\t\n41\t## Criterion Details\n42\t\n43\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n44\tAll seven HLRs map to at least one epic in the traceability matrix: HLR-1 (E001+E002), HLR-2/3/4/5 (E001), HLR-6 (E001), HLR-7 (E003). All five P0 HLRs covered. Priority inheritance is correct: E001 blocks all P0 work and is P0; E002 (P0) depends only on E001 (P0); E003 is a docs-authoring epic whose P0 derives from the strategy's `priority: Critical` via the docs-priority rule — exempt from priority inheritance and correctly explained in Health Warnings. No priority collapse: HLR-6 (P1, standalone-path observability) shares E001's controller status-condition surface — incidental to the P0 work, not a deferrable separate feature, so bundling is correct. Minor: HLR-6's WVA-path half (WVA events) is delegated to RHAISTRAT-7670 with an explicit, reasonable justification (the WVA path is unchanged by this strategy) and is flagged in Health Warnings — the split ownership should be confirmed against RHAISTRAT-7670's scope, but it does not leave this strategy's deliverables unmapped.\n45\t\n46\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n47\tNo circular dependencies. Edges: E001→E002 (Rule 13 build-against artifact — E002's reconciler compiles against and reconciles the new v1alpha2 `spec.scaling.keda` API types; a genuine compile-time artifact dependency, not an image-digest/endpoint configuration reference), E001→E003 and E002→E003 (Rule 11 docs-last — the docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic, verified by the contract validator). Diagram arrows follow the dependency→dependent convention and match frontmatter `dependencies` exactly in both directions (E002: [E001]; E003: [E001, E002]; E001: []). Critical path length 3 matches the M-strategy heuristic (3-4). No konflux-onboarding/repo-onboarding/license-validation epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are not applicable; the E001→E002 serialization of two-repo work is justified by the shared API artifact. No conditional branch files exist, so branch field requirements do not apply. Cross-check of epic content found no data/artifact dependency missing from the DAG: E003's TriggerAuthentication and recipe content is gated on both implementing epics.\n48\t\n49\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n50\tThree distinct component/team tuples produce three epics: (Serving Orchestration, Serving team), (Model Runtimes, Serving team), (Documentation, Documentation team). Canonical component names validated against `.context/rhai-components.txt` by the contract validator; the two non-obvious mappings (KServe llmisvc-controller→Serving Orchestration, odh-model-controller→Model Runtimes) are documented with rationale. No scope duplication: E001 creates ScaledObjects in kserve, E002 creates per-namespace auth resources in odh-model-controller — complementary surfaces; the shared RBAC/TLS open-question work is explicitly split (E001: \"beyond the authentication context\"; E002: \"for the authentication context\"), which is a division of one unknown, not duplicate deliverables. No work-product type mixing — E003 is pure content authoring. Minor: E001 bundles the KServe implementation (strategy estimate 1-2 sprints) with cross-topology integration testing (0.5 sprint), which reaches ~2.5 sprints at the top of the estimate — at/above the ~2-week guideline, acceptable only because upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 supplies a reference implementation and the work is one cohesive API+validation+controller change that cannot ship in independent halves.\n51\t\n52\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n53\tAll three epics are Implementation; E003 correctly carries `implementation_type: docs-authoring`. The decision to create no Investigation epics is verified against the \"does the answer change which downstream epics exist\" test for all three strategy open questions: (1) upstream PR #5839 adaptation changes in-epic scope with a defined fallback (carry the patch downstream), correctly modeled as an acceptance criterion on E001 per the third-party-resolves pattern rather than a gate; (2) scale-from-zero SLO and (3) Prometheus capacity guidance change documentation content only, captured as E003 ACs/notes. The \"needs validation\" assumptions (idleReplicaCount pause/resume, EPP metrics at zero replicas) are behavior validations with fix-and-retry semantics, captured as E001 ACs. All `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are null, consistent with the absence of gates; no epic lists an Investigation dependency.\n54\t\n55\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n56\tEach epic carries the correct signal set for its type (ai_signals on all three Implementation epics; investigation_signals null). Signal values are consistent with the strategy content and verified rationales: E001 external_dependency -1 (upstream PR timing outside RHOAI control) vs E002 external_dependency 0 (mirrors an existing in-repo pattern; KEDA/Prometheus are existing cluster prerequisites) is a justified distinction; E001 open_questions 0 is defensible because the one open question has a defined in-epic fallback that does not change the approach; E003 open_questions -1 (two unresolved content inputs from llm-d and Performance/PSAP teams) and human_process_gates -1 (mandatory engineering technical review before publication) match the epic's own review AC. architecture_claims: 1 verified — the cited context files (kserve.md, odh-model-controller.md, workload-variant-autoscaler.md) exist and document the claimed behavior (llmisvc controller managing KEDA ScaledObjects; odh-model-controller creating KEDA TriggerAuthentications). Signal rationales in all three ai-signals files match the frontmatter values. Per instructions, computed scores were not re-checked.\n57\t\n58\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n59\tAll epics have testable, strategy-derived ACs. Rule-mandated ACs are present: E003 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review AC; backwards-compatibility ACs appear in both implementation epics (E001: unchanged WVA-path integration suite; E002: unmodified v1beta1 kserve_keda_reconciler tests); no konflux chain exists so the build-pipeline AC is not applicable. No non-testable schedule-constraint ACs — the external-input ACs (upstream PR path, SLO/capacity inputs) each define a verifiable fallback (carry patch with backport plan; ship with the RFE-referenced 60-second p50 flagged for update), so they are testable rather than misfiled risk notes, and the external-input risks are additionally carried in Health Warnings/E003 Notes. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID.\n60\t\n61\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n62\tFull scope coverage: two-path API/validation/controller work, scale-to/from-zero, disaggregated scaling, upstream integration and fallback (E001); OpenShift TriggerAuthentication flow with lifecycle cleanup (E002); decision guide, per-workload recipes, scale-to/from-zero guidance with ScaledObject templates, authenticated Prometheus triggers, troubleshooting, supported configuration matrix, KEDA prerequisite (overlay 0008 / ODH-ADR-0007), cold-start expectations, and fleet-scale capacity guidance (E003). All seven strategy acceptance criteria appear verbatim-or-equivalent in E001's AC list. Strategy risks, assumptions, and open questions are carried forward rather than dropped (each maps to an epic AC, note, or health warning). Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\" rather than \"Yes\", but a docs-authoring epic exists regardless, satisfying HLR-7. Minor: the Observability NFR's \"