s to RHAISTRAT-7670, where WVA-mediated autoscaling remains the technical path; this decomposition covers the standalone-KEDA half (ScaledObject events / scaling status conditions) in E001. The P1 scaling-observability work is bundled in E001 rather than split: it shares the same controller status-condition surface as the P0 path implementation, so it is incidental to the P0 work, not a deferrable feature on a separate surface.\n66\t\n67\t## Component Mappings\n68\t\n69\tCanonical component names are taken verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`. Non-obvious mappings:\n70\t\n71\t| Strategy component | Canonical component | Note |\n72\t|--------------------|---------------------|------|\n73\t| KServe (llmisvc-controller) | Serving Orchestration | No literal \"KServe\" entry in the canonical list; the llmisvc-controller is the serving orchestration layer for LLMInferenceService. |\n74\t| odh-model-controller | Model Runtimes | No literal \"odh-model-controller\" entry; the controller manages model-serving runtime deployment concerns (including KEDA auth for serving workloads). |\n75\t| Documentation | Documentation | Verbatim match. |\n76\t\n77\t## Unknowns Analysis (Step 3)\n78\t\n79\tAll unknowns were collected from the Open Questions table, Risks, and Assumptions before evaluation:\n80\t\n81\t1. **Does upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 require downstream adaptation beyond the authentication context?** (Owner: KServe team) — The answer changes in-epic scope (possible OpenShift RBAC/TLS adjustments) but not which epics exist: the adaptation work naturally falls inside E001's integration scope (rebase or carry-patch), and E002 already owns the authentication context. Resolved by doing the rebase — captured as an acceptance criterion on E001, not an Investigation epic.\n82\t2. **Target p50/p95 for scale-from-zero time** (Owner: llm-d team) — A product SLO that determines documentation content (headroom pre-scaling recommendations), not epic structure. Captured as an input/AC on E003; flagged as a health warning (external input with a 60-second p50 reference from the RFE).\n83\t3. **Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet-scale deployments** (Owner: Performance/PSAP team) — Documentation content, not structure. Captured as an AC on E003; flagged as a health warning (external PSAP input).\n84\t\n85\tAssumptions marked \"needs validation\" (KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0` pause/resume behavior; EPP flow control metrics availability at zero replicas) are validations of behavior during implementation — failure means fix-and-retry, not different epics. Captured as acceptance criteria on E001 (scale-to-zero and metric-outage ACs from the strategy).\n86\t\n87\t## Health Warnings\n88\t\n89\t- **Pending process gates**: Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and other Prerequisites are all \"Pending human review\" — the strategy is in Refinement. Decomposition assumes sign-off; a materially different review outcome would require re-decomposition.\n90\t- **Upstream timing risk**: E001 depends on kserve/kserve#5839 (open, assigned). Fallback (carry the patch downstream with backport) is an explicit AC on E001; if the PR is rejected rather than delayed, E001's approach needs rework — monitor upstream.\n91\t- **External inputs to documentation**: E003 content depends on inputs the Documentation team does not control (scale-from-zero SLO from the llm-d team; Prometheus capacity guidance from Performance/PSAP). If unavailable, E003 ships with the RFE-referenced 60-second p50 and a capacity-tuning section marked as guidance pending PSAP validation.\n92\t- **Docs priority**: E003 is P0 solely via the docs-authoring priority rule (strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0); its underlying HLR is P1. Not a priority inversion — rule-driven — but planners should treat it as P1-value work sequenced last.\n93\t- **HLR-6 split ownership**: the WVA-events half of the observability HLR is owned by RHAISTRAT-7670; only the standalone-KE