{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b540ac061597dd53","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8008\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8008\n11\t\n12\tInference-Aware Autoscaling for llm-d — standalone KEDA primitives for LLMInferenceService.\n13\t\n14\tStaff Engineer / SME Input section is empty (template placeholder only) — no overrides to apply.\n15\t\n16\t## Epic List\n17\t\n18\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n19\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-8008-E001 | Standalone KEDA autoscaling path for LLMInferenceService in kserve llmisvc-controller | Implementation | Serving team | P0 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-8008-E002 | KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService in odh-model-controller | Implementation | Serving team | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-8008-E003 | Autoscaling decision guide and configuration documentation for LLMInferenceService | Implementation (docs-authoring) | Documentation team | P0 |\n23\t\n24\tNote on E001 sizing: E001 bundles the KServe implementation (strategy estimate 1-2 sprints) with cross-topology integration testing (0.5 sprint), reaching ~2.5 sprints at the top of the estimate — at/above the ~2-week epic guideline. It is kept as a single epic deliberately: the API, validation, and controller change is one cohesive deliverable that cannot ship in independent halves, and upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 supplies a reference implementation for the bulk of the code.\n25\t\n26\t## Dependency DAG\n27\t\n28\t```mermaid\n29\tgraph TD\n30\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-8008-E001 Standalone KEDA path in llmisvc-controller]\n31\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-8008-E002 TriggerAuthentication reconciler in odh-model-controller]\n32\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-8008-E003 Autoscaling documentation]\n33\t    E001 --> E002\n34\t    E001 --> E003\n35\t    E002 --> E003\n36\t```\n37\t\n38\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E003 (length 3).\n39\t\n40\t## DAG Justification\n41\t\n42\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n43\t|------|------|-----------|\n44\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | E001 lands the standalone KEDA spec in the LLMInferenceService v1alpha2 API (ScalingSpec `keda` field, CEL validation). The odh-model-controller reconciler's code builds against those API types and reconciles CRs carrying the new fields — a build-against API/CRD artifact, not a configuration reference. |\n45\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes the implemented API and behavior; blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n46\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must cover the OpenShift TriggerAuthentication flow delivered by the odh-model-controller work. |\n47\t\n48\tNotes on edges deliberately NOT drawn:\n49\t\n50\t- No Investigation epics were created (see Unknowns Analysis below). All three strategy open questions fail the \"does the answer change which downstream epics exist or what they do\" test — they change content or in-epic scope, not epic structure.\n51\t- Upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 is modeled as a precondition with a defined fallback (carry the patch downstream), per Rule 21 third-party-resolves case — an acceptance criterion on E001, not a separate upstream epic.\n52\t- No konflux-onboarding / repo-onboarding / license-validation epics: kserve and odh-model-controller are existing platform components with architecture context files; the change extends existing code in already-onboarded repos.\n53\t\n54\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n55\t\n56\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n57\t|-----|----------|---------|\n58\t| HLR-1: Standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers without WVA | P0 | E001, E002 |\n59\t| HLR-2: Mutual exclusivity of WVA and standalone KEDA paths (CEL validation) | P0 | E001 |\n60\t| HLR-3: Scale-to-zero via KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0` (both paths) | P0 | E001 |\n61\t| HLR-4: Scale-from-zero (standalone KEDA via user-configured triggers) | P0 | E001 |\n62\t| HLR-5: Independent prefill/decode scaling in disaggregated deployments | P0 | E001 |\n63\t| HLR-6: Scaling events with signal attribution | P1 | E001 |\n64\t| HLR-7: Decision-guide documentation covering both paths | P1 | E003 |\n65\t\n66\tFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n67\t\n68\tHLR-6 note: the WVA-path half of this HLR (WVA events) belongs to RHAISTRAT-7670, where WVA-mediated autoscaling remains the technical path; this decomposition covers the standalone-KEDA half (ScaledObject events / scaling status conditions) in E001. The P1 scaling-observability work is bundled in E001 rather than split: it shares the same controller status-condition surface as the P0 path implementation, so it is incidental to the P0 work, not a deferrable feature on a separate surface. Split-ownership confirmation: RHAISTRAT-7670's scope should be confirmed to explicitly cover WVA scaling-event visibility so HLR-6 is fully closed across the two strategies. The WVA-path scaling status conditions named in the strategy's Affected Components are assigned to E001's backwards-compatibility scope (preserved by default; updated in E001 if the shared status-condition surface changes), so that half is never unassigned.\n69\t\n70\tObservability NFR note: the \"scaling metrics exposed through KEDA ScaledObject metrics for dashboard integration\" half of the Observability NFR is inherent KEDA behavior — KEDA exposes ScaledObject scaler metric values natively via its own metrics server, and RHOAI neither installs nor modifies KEDA (platform policy, overlay 0008) — so it requires no RHOAI-side epic work. Only the events half (ScaledObject events and status conditions) carries work, in E001.\n71\t\n72\t## Component Mappings\n73\t\n74\tCanonical component names are taken verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`. Non-obvious mappings